'''Construccions de Sant Cristòfor, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Construccions de Sant Cristòfor}}), commonly known as '''CSC''', is a Balboan construction and real estate development company and the country's largest residential developer. Headquartered in the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] of [[Sant Cristòfor]], the company designs, builds, and sells apartment buildings and mixed-use developments across the Balboan isthmus. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker CSC.

CSC was founded in 1962, during the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor]] period, by two engineers — [[Jordi Estrada i Soler]] and [[Pau Vidal i Roure]] — who had worked together in the mandate-era public works department. The company was originally a small construction firm focused on residential building in the Sant Cristòfor metropolitan area, but it expanded over the following decades into a national developer with projects across the isthmus.

The company is known for its focus on mid-rise apartment buildings and mixed-use developments, which have become a defining feature of the Balboan urban landscape. CSC developments are characterised by their modernist architecture, their integration of ground-floor retail with residential upper floors, and their emphasis on community amenities — courtyards, rooftop gardens, and shared spaces.

== History ==

=== Founding and early years (1962–1975) ===

Construccions de Sant Cristòfor was founded in 1962 by [[Jordi Estrada i Soler]] and [[Pau Vidal i Roure]], two civil engineers who had met while working in the public works department of the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor]]. The mandate period had produced a construction boom in Sant Cristòfor, as the international administration invested in infrastructure and housing to accommodate the growing population of the canal corridor, and Estrada and Vidal recognised the opportunity to establish a private construction firm.

The company's first project was a 24-unit apartment building in the [[El Comerç]] district of [[Barcelona (ciutat)|Barcelona]], completed in 1963. The building — a six-storey concrete structure with ground-floor retail and five floors of apartments above — established the template that would define CSC's developments for the next six decades: mid-rise, mixed-use, community-anchored residential buildings.

The early years were focused on the Sant Cristòfor metropolitan area, where the post-war housing shortage and the influx of workers to the canal corridor created strong demand for apartment construction. The company built a reputation for quality construction and timely delivery, and it became one of the preferred developers for the mandate-era housing programmes.

=== Post-reunification expansion (1975–1995) ===

The reunification of Balboa in 1975 opened the interior to private development, and CSC was among the first Sant Cristòfor-based companies to expand into the interior cities. The company opened a regional office in Sant Bart in 1976 and began developing apartment buildings in the interior's growing urban centres.

The [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis]] of the late 1980s and the [[1994 Balboa spot recession]] tested the company's resilience. The crisis was driven by a speculative bubble in real estate, and many developers collapsed when the bubble burst. CSC, which had maintained a conservative balance sheet and had focused on mid-rise residential construction rather than the speculative luxury developments that had driven the bubble, survived the crisis in a stronger competitive position. The company's reputation for quality and its conservative financial management made it a favoured developer for the post-crisis recovery, and it acquired several distressed properties from failed developers at favourable prices.

The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1994, at the trough of the spot recession. The listing raised capital that was used to fund the company's expansion into the Estret corridor, where the post-crisis recovery was generating demand for new housing.

=== Diversification and growth (1995–2020) ===

The late 1990s and 2000s saw CSC expand its portfolio beyond pure residential construction into mixed-use developments that combined residential, commercial, and office space. The company's mixed-use developments — which typically included ground-floor retail, several floors of office space, and upper floors of apartments — became a defining feature of the Estret corridor's urban landscape, particularly in the Dàrsena and Barcelona districts of Sant Cristòfor.

The company also expanded its geographic reach, developing projects in Portnou, Sant Llàtzer, and the smaller interior cities. By 2010, CSC had built over 15,000 residential units across the isthmus and had become the largest residential developer in Balboa.

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 temporarily halted construction activity, but the pandemic produced a surge in demand for residential housing as urban residents sought larger apartments with home office space and outdoor access. CSC's focus on mid-rise apartments with balconies and community amenities positioned the company well for the post-pandemic housing market, and the company's revenue and earnings grew strongly in the 2021–2024 period.

=== Modern era (2020–present) ===

By 2026, CSC employed approximately 8,600 people and had a portfolio of 42 active development projects across the isthmus, including 3,200 residential units under construction. The company's completed portfolio exceeded 28,000 residential units across 180 buildings, making it the largest residential developer in Balboan history.

The company's recent developments have focused on the Dàrsena district of Sant Cristòfor, where the conversion of former industrial and warehouse properties into mixed-use residential developments has been a major driver of the district's urban renaissance, and on the growing interior cities, where demand for modern housing has outpaced supply.

== Operations ==

CSC operates across three principal business lines:

* '''Residential development''': The company's core business, designing, building, and selling apartment buildings across the Balboan isthmus. The company focuses on mid-rise buildings (5 to 12 storeys) with ground-floor retail and community amenities. CSC developments are concentrated in the Sant Cristòfor metropolitan area (60 per cent of the portfolio), the Estret corridor (25 per cent), and the interior cities (15 per cent).
* '''Mixed-use development''': Developments that combine residential apartments with commercial retail, office space, and community facilities. The company's mixed-use projects are typically larger than its pure residential developments, ranging from 80 to 300 units, and are concentrated in the urban centres of Sant Cristòfor, Portnou, and Sant Llàtzer.
* '''Property management''': The company manages a portfolio of approximately 4,200 rental units in buildings that it has developed and retained, providing a stable recurring revenue stream that complements the cyclical development business.

The company's headquarters are at 140 Carrer Frederic, in the Districte Financer of Sant Cristòfor, and it maintains regional offices in Portnou, Sant Bart, and Varakí.

